{"units": [{"id": "palos-quote6", "translation": "as each drew the lot of his fortune"}, {"id": "palos-quote7", "translation": "cast the lots from the helmets as quickly as possible"}, {"id": "palos-quote8", "translation": "for the number of the lots is equal"}, {"id": "palton-orth1", "translation": "javelin, missile"}, {"id": "palton-def1", "translation": "javelin, missile"}, {"id": "palton-note1", "translation": "The grammarians explain it by ἀκόντιον [javelin]: see Thes. vol. 6 p. 109."}, {"id": "palton-quote1", "translation": "and hurling javelins and thonged spears and a stone"}, {"id": "palton-word1", "translation": "javelin"}, {"id": "pambotos-orth1", "translation": "nourishing all"}, {"id": "pambotos-def1", "translation": "nourishing all"}, {"id": "pambotos-quote1", "translation": "the all-nourishing grove of Zeus"}, {"id": "pammataios-orth1", "translation": "utterly vain"}, {"id": "pammataios-def1", "translation": "utterly vain"}, {"id": "pammataios-note1", "translation": "where Musgrave rightly corrected to πᾶν μάταιον [all in vain]."}, {"id": "pammataios-quote1", "translation": "a remedy utterly vain"}, {"id": "pammataios-word1", "translation": "all in vain"}, {"id": "pammachos-orth1", "translation": "fighting with all"}, {"id": "pammachos-def1", "translation": "fighting with all"}, {"id": "pammachos-quote1", "translation": "teeming with all-fighting boldness"}, {"id": "pammeter-orth1", "translation": "mother of all"}, {"id": "pammeter-def1", "translation": "mother of all"}, {"id": "pammeter-quote1", "translation": "and Earth, mother of all"}, {"id": "pammiges-orth1", "translation": "mixed from all"}, {"id": "pammiges-def1", "translation": "mixed from all"}, {"id": "pammiges-def2", "translation": "in the same sense"}, {"id": "pammiges-note1", "translation": "i.e. the weapons of an army composed of many peoples. Schol. διαφόρων ἐθνῶν [of different nations]."}, {"id": "pammiges-quote1", "translation": "the many missiles, mixed from all"}, {"id": "pammiges-quote2", "translation": "a mixed crowd"}, {"id": "pammiges-quote3", "translation": "and of mixed allies"}, {"id": "pammiges-word1", "translation": "of different nations"}, {"id": "pampedos-orth1", "translation": "utterly, completely"}, {"id": "pampedos-def1", "translation": "utterly, completely"}, {"id": "pampedos-note1", "translation": "It is a poetic word, which the grammarians explain by παντελῶς [completely]: see Thes. vol. 6 p. 115."}, {"id": "pampedos-quote1", "translation": "has the whole people thus been utterly destroyed by the spear?"}, {"id": "pampedos-quote2", "translation": "when he wishes to utterly ruin a house"}, {"id": "pampedos-word1", "translation": "completely"}, {"id": "pampesia-orth1", "translation": "entire possession"}, {"id": "pampesia-def1", "translation": "entire possession"}, {"id": "pampesia-note1", "translation": "Photius p. 375, 12 and Eustathius p. 1502, 48 explain it by παγκτησία [entire property]; Hesychius by πᾶσα ἡ ὕπαρξις [all one's substance]. τὴν περιουσίαν [the abundance] is a gloss in M."}, {"id": "pampesia-quote1", "translation": "they divided - the entire possession of the property"}, {"id": "pampesia-word1", "translation": "entire property"}, {"id": "pampesia-word2", "translation": "all one's substance"}, {"id": "pampesia-word3", "translation": "the abundance"}, {"id": "pampreptos-orth1", "translation": "conspicuous from afar, most splendid"}, {"id": "pampreptos-def1", "translation": "conspicuous from afar, most splendid"}, {"id": "pampreptos-quote1", "translation": "in most conspicuous seats"}, {"id": "pamprosthe-orth1", "translation": "the same as παντελῶς πρόσθε [utterly before]"}, {"id": "pamprosthe-def1", "translation": "the same as παντελῶς πρόσθε [utterly before]"}, {"id": "pamprosthe-note1", "translation": "Thus Hermann for παμπρόσθη [utterly before], with Heath preceding, who had conjectured πάμπροσθεν [utterly before]. It refers to the siege of Troy protracted over a long time, as Paleius noted."}, {"id": "pamprosthe-quote1", "translation": "utterly before, she -"}, {"id": "pamprosthe-word1", "translation": "utterly before"}, {"id": "pamprosthe-word2", "translation": "utterly before"}, {"id": "pamprosthe-word3", "translation": "utterly before"}, {"id": "pamphaes-orth1", "translation": "illuminating all things with its light, translucent"}, {"id": "pamphaes-def1", "translation": "illuminating all things with its light, translucent"}, {"id": "pamphaes-note1", "translation": "The scholiast explains it by λαμπρὸν, καθαρόν [bright, pure]."}, {"id": "pamphaes-quote1", "translation": "all-shining honey"}, {"id": "pamphaes-word1", "translation": "bright, pure"}, {"id": "pamphthartos-orth1", "translation": "utterly destructive"}, {"id": "pamphthartos-def1", "translation": "utterly destructive"}, {"id": "pamphthartos-quote1", "translation": "with utterly destructive fate"}, {"id": "pamphoros-orth1", "translation": "bearing or producing all things"}, {"id": "pamphoros-def1", "translation": "bearing or producing all things"}, {"id": "pamphoros-quote1", "translation": "children of the all-bearing earth"}, {"id": "pamphyloi-orth1", "translation": "Pamphylians, a people of Asia Minor"}, {"id": "pamphyloi-def1", "translation": "Pamphylians, a people of Asia Minor"}, {"id": "pamphyloi-note1", "translation": "Thus Heath for Παμφύλων τε γένη [and the races of the Pamphylians]."}, {"id": "pamphyloi-quote1", "translation": "and through the mountains of the Cilicians and Pamphylians, rushing over the ever-flowing rivers of the land"}, {"id": "pamphyloi-word1", "translation": "and the races of the Pamphylians"}, {"id": "pan-orth1", "translation": "Pan, deity of shepherds"}, {"id": "pan-def1", "translation": "Pan, deity of shepherds"}, {"id": "pan-def2", "translation": "in the plural Πάνες [Pans]"}, {"id": "pan-note1", "translation": "Casaubonus added ἀκρατεῖς [unrestrained] from the lower scholium in his work on satyr poetry 1, 5."}, {"id": "pan-note2", "translation": "Vaterus p. 86 has Ἀρκάδος [of Arcas] for ὃν καὶ [whom also]."}, {"id": "pan-quote1", "translation": "there is an island - which Pan, lover of the chorus, haunts"}, {"id": "pan-quote2", "translation": "or some Apollo or Pan or Zeus"}, {"id": "pan-quote3", "translation": "most say that the Satyrs, like the Sileni and Pans, as Aeschylus in Glaucus, and Sophocles in Andromeda"}, {"id": "pan-quote4", "translation": "Aeschylus speaks of two Pans, one the son of Zeus, whom he also calls twin, and the other of Cronus"}, {"id": "pan-word1", "translation": "Pans"}, {"id": "pan-word2", "translation": "unrestrained"}, {"id": "pan-word3", "translation": "of Arcas"}, {"id": "pan-word4", "translation": "whom also"}, {"id": "panathlios-orth1", "translation": "utterly wretched, most miserable"}, {"id": "panathlios-def1", "translation": "utterly wretched, most miserable"}, {"id": "panathlios-quote1", "translation": "and you, utterly wretched one"}, {"id": "panathlios-quote2", "translation": "my head, beaten and utterly wretched"}, {"id": "panathlios-quote3", "translation": "you strip me of my friends, utterly wretched as I am"}, {"id": "panaiolos-orth1", "translation": "utterly varied, manifold"}, {"id": "panaiolos-def1", "translation": "utterly varied, manifold"}, {"id": "panaiolos-quote1", "translation": "the utterly varied, mournful, ill-sounding utterances"}, {"id": "panaitios-orth1", "translation": "author of the whole matter"}, {"id": "panaitios-def1", "translation": "author of the whole matter"}, {"id": "panaitios-note1", "translation": "thus Canter for εἰς [into]; thus Wakefield for ὡς [as]."}, {"id": "panaitios-quote1", "translation": "through Zeus, the all-causing, all-working"}, {"id": "panaitios-quote2", "translation": "but you did everything, being the author of all"}, {"id": "panaitios-word1", "translation": "into"}, {"id": "panaitios-word2", "translation": "as"}, {"id": "panalethes-orth1", "translation": "utterly true, most true"}, {"id": "panalethes-def1", "translation": "utterly true, most true"}, {"id": "panalethes-note1", "translation": "I restored the Doric form παναλαθή [utterly true]."}]}